Consider the unconstrained optimization problem
   min { f(x) : x ∈ ℝⁿ },
where f : ℝⁿ → ℝ is continuously differentiable and bounded below, with gradient g(x) = ∇f(x). A nonlinear conjugate gradient (CG) algorithm generates iterates x_{k+1} = x_k + α_k d_k, where the search direction is d_0 = −g_0 and, for k ≥ 1,
   d_k = −g_k + β_k d_{k−1},
for some scalar β_k (e.g., Fletcher–Reeves, Polak–Ribière–Polyak, Hestenes–Stiefel, Dai–Yuan, Hager–Zhang, etc.). The step length α_k > 0 is typically obtained by a line-search procedure, examples being:
 (i) exact line search: α_k = argmin_{α>0} f(x_k + α d_k);
 (ii) standard Wolfe conditions: f(x_k+α_k d_k) ≤ f(x_k) + c_1 α_k g_k^T d_k and g(x_k+α_k d_k)^T d_k ≥ c_2 g_k^T d_k, with 0 < c_1 < c_2 < 1;
 (iii) approximate Wolfe conditions of Hager–Zhang;
 (iv) non-monotone line-search conditions (e.g., Grippo–Lampariello–Lucidi type), in which the Armijo-type acceptance test compares f(x_k+α_k d_k) to max_{0≤j≤m(k)} f(x_{k−j}) instead of f(x_k).

Address the following questions rigorously:

(a) Quantify the influence of the accuracy of the line-search procedure on the convergence speed, robustness, and overall numerical performance of nonlinear CG algorithms. In particular, identify which level and which type of line-search accuracy among (i)–(iv) is most effective for a representative class of smooth (possibly nonconvex) unconstrained problems.

(b) Determine whether the non-monotone line search of type (iv) is more effective, in terms of efficiency and reliability, than the (standard or approximate) Wolfe line search of types (ii)–(iii) when used inside nonlinear CG schemes.

(c) Design a competitive nonlinear CG algorithm for unconstrained minimization that completely avoids any line-search procedure (i.e., uses a closed-form or otherwise computationally cheap rule for α_k that does not require evaluating f or g at trial points along d_k), prove its global convergence under standard assumptions (e.g., Lipschitz continuous gradient, bounded level sets), and show that it is competitive with state-of-the-art line-search-based CG methods on standard benchmarks.

