Nuclear fission reactors produce a flux of electron antineutrinos $\bar\nu_e$ from the beta decays of fission fragments of the principal fissile isotopes ${}^{235}\mathrm{U}$, ${}^{238}\mathrm{U}$, ${}^{239}\mathrm{Pu}$, and ${}^{241}\mathrm{Pu}$. Standard summation and conversion calculations (e.g., the Huber–Mueller model) predict an absolute $\bar\nu_e$ emission rate and energy spectrum per fission. Short-baseline experiments that detect reactor antineutrinos via the inverse beta-decay reaction $\bar\nu_e + p \to e^+ + n$ consistently measure an integrated flux that is approximately $94\%$ of the predicted value, a roughly $6\%$ deficit known as the reactor antineutrino anomaly.

Determine the origin of this deficit. Specifically, decide among the following (mutually non-exclusive) possibilities and quantify the contribution of each:
(i) new physics beyond the Standard Model, in particular oscillations of $\bar\nu_e$ into one or more light sterile neutrino states $\nu_s$ with mass-squared splitting $\Delta m^2 \sim 1\,\mathrm{eV}^2$ and mixing parameter $\sin^2 2\theta$ to be determined;
(ii) systematic experimental errors in the absolute normalization of the short-baseline measurements;
(iii) errors in the theoretical reactor antineutrino flux calculations, in particular in the modeling of the beta-decay branches of fission fragments of ${}^{235}\mathrm{U}$ (and possibly other isotopes).

Provide a quantitative explanation of the observed $\sim 6\%$ deficit and of the energy-dependent spectral distortion (the "5 MeV bump") seen in the measured spectrum.

