Design and carry out an experimental test of a Bell inequality that simultaneously closes the principal experimental loopholes — in particular the locality (communication) loophole, the detection (fair-sampling) loophole, and the freedom-of-choice loophole — and which yields a statistically significant violation of a Bell-type inequality (e.g., the CHSH inequality), thereby refuting the class of local hidden-variable theories.

Concretely: consider two spatially separated measurement stations A and B, each receiving one particle of an entangled pair. At each run, an independent random number generator at each station selects a measurement setting (a ∈ {a₁, a₂} at A; b ∈ {b₁, b₂} at B), producing binary outcomes A(a), B(b) ∈ {−1, +1}. Let
  S = E(a₁,b₁) + E(a₁,b₂) + E(a₂,b₁) − E(a₂,b₂),
where E(a,b) denotes the correlation of outcomes. Any local hidden-variable model satisfies |S| ≤ 2. The task is to produce an experimental realization in which:
 (i) the choice of setting at each station and the registration of the outcome at the other station are space-like separated (locality loophole closed);
 (ii) all entangled pairs prepared are accounted for in the statistics so that no fair-sampling assumption is required (detection loophole closed);
 (iii) the setting choices are made by independent, suitably random processes (freedom-of-choice loophole closed);
and in which the measured value of S violates the bound |S| ≤ 2 with high statistical significance.

