Propose a uniform, stakeholder-neutral definition of "engineering/design deliverable quality" for capital construction projects, together with an associated quantitative measurement methodology. The definition and measurement method should satisfy the following requirements:

1. Applicability: It must be meaningful and usable by all of the following classes of project stakeholders: project owners, constructors and craft labor, equipment suppliers, and engineers/designers.

2. Definition: Provide a precise specification of what constitutes an "engineering/design deliverable" (e.g., drawings, specifications, models, calculations, datasheets) and what aspects of such a deliverable count toward its "quality" (e.g., accuracy, completeness, clarity, timeliness, constructability, consistency across disciplines, compliance with standards).

3. Measurement method: Define a procedure that assigns to any given engineering/design deliverable D a quality score Q(D) (which may be scalar- or vector-valued) such that:
   (a) the inputs to the procedure are observable or auditable attributes of D;
   (b) the procedure is reproducible, in the sense that two independent evaluators applying it to the same D obtain scores within a stated tolerance;
   (c) the scoring scale is calibrated so that scores are comparable across deliverables of different types and across different projects;
   (d) the method admits aggregation rules to produce package-level or project-level quality measures from individual deliverable scores.

4. Validation criteria: State the criteria under which the proposed definition and measurement method would be considered validated (for example, statistical correlation of Q(D) with downstream rework, change orders, schedule slippage, or cost overruns).

The task is to construct such a definition Q, the associated measurement methodology, and the validation framework, and to demonstrate that the construction meets requirements (1)–(4).

